From: lccc-agent <agent@arena.invalid>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 20:30:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 04/38] link: DT_INIT/DT_FINI as GNU ld,
 -nostartfiles/-nodefaultlibs forwarded, no default-library guessing, import
 binding

Measured against GCC 16.2 / GCC 14 cross + bfd on all four targets
(tests/regression/check_builtin_dso_link.sh, every leg now also checks
the items below; 1.6 s total):

- DT_INIT/DT_FINI exist iff the image defines _init/_fini and record that
  symbol (GNU ld's -init/-fini default).  AArch64 emitted neither tag for
  executables or DSOs, so legacy .init/.fini fragments (crti's frame,
  glibc's __gmon_start__ hook) never ran; x86-64/i686 keyed the tag on the
  .init SECTION, so a -nostartfiles DSO with a bare .init fragment (no
  prologue, no ret) got a DT_INIT the loader would run off the end of.
  Presence is decided once from definedness (linker_common::
  image_init_fini) before layout: a first AArch64 version tested the
  value, which is 0 before layout, under-sized .dynamic and clobbered
  DT_FLAGS_1/DT_NULL -- caught by the dlopen leg, fixed by construction.
  i686 uses its DynamicDesc presence/fill split.  RISC-V needs none (its
  glibc crti has no .init; bfd emits no tag either).
- -nostartfiles / -nodefaultlibs were recognised by the CLI and dropped:
  build_linker_args never forwarded them, so backend::common's handling
  was dead (crt1 still linked -- a freestanding _start collided with
  crt1's; -shared -nostartfiles still got crtbeginS).  Now Driver fields,
  forwarded; unit test pins parse -> linker args.
- x86-64 and AArch64 lccc-ld resolved leftover undefined symbols against
  libc/libm/libgcc_s/ld-linux even with no default libraries requested:
  -nostdlib / -nodefaultlibs code calling puts linked (GCC: undefined
  reference; i686/RISC-V already failed), and gcc -B<lccc-ld> without -lm
  linked where bfd fails.  The fallback now runs only when the driver
  requested gcc's default libraries.
- An import's .dynsym binding is STB_WEAK iff every reference is weak
  (linker_common::import_strong_refs, shared with x86-64): AArch64 copied
  the provider's binding -- crtbeginS's weak-only __cxa_finalize became
  GLOBAL, and a strong call to getc/fgetc (WEAK in glibc) became a WEAK
  import that ld.so may bind to 0.
