From: lccc-agent <agent@arena.invalid>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 20:30:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 12/38] x86 FP copies without false dependences; upward-exposed
 slot reads; deferred LEB128; errexit-negation detector

Code generation (x86-64)
- Register-to-register scalar FP copies use the full-register packed move
  (`movapd`/`movaps`, `vmovapd`/`vmovaps`). A legacy `movsd %a, %d` merges
  and so reads %d's previous value (in a loop, the previous iteration's
  result); `vmovsd %a, %a, %d` is a port-5 shuffle that is not eliminated
  at rename. The packed move is GCC's spelling and is move-eliminated on
  Intel since Ivy Bridge and on AMD Zen. Scalar consumers ignore the upper
  lanes; `ret` readers of %xmm0 are respected through LCCC_RET_XMM_BITS.
- The `movsd`+`vOPsd` fusion accepts the packed copy for either consumer
  width; a `movss` copy feeding an `sd` consumer is refused (it defines only
  bits 31:0 of the destination).
- The FMA bracket rewrite treats a replacing copy-out (`movapd`, VEX copy)
  under the upper-bits rule, and `ret` as a reader of %xmm0.

Peephole correctness and precision
- Stack-slot moves name the base they actually use (`%rbp` or `%rsp`).
  The bit used to be hard-coded to `%rbp` for both, so in a frameless
  function that allocates `%rbp` as a GPR every `%rsp` spill was a phantom
  read of `%rbp`: liveness kept the value alive across whole loops and
  blocked folds (`zlib_ng_adler32_combine`: 7 -> 6 instructions per
  iteration).
- Dead-store elimination attributes a slot load fully covered by earlier
  stores in its own straight-line run to exactly those stores (the classic
  upward-exposed-use refinement) instead of pinning every store to the slot
  in the function. It answers "unknown" at labels, jumps, calls, inline asm,
  %rsp shifts, other frame memory operands and cross-base slot stores.
- The relay/LEA window counts instructions only, so `.loc` lines no longer
  shorten it and `-g` no longer changes code.

Assemblers
- AArch64 `.uleb128`/`.sleb128` of a later-resolved label difference
  (`.gcc_except_table`, DWARF lengths) are reserved and resolved after
  layout, re-assembling with the measured widths until they converge. The
  widths only grow (at most 10 bytes each), so the loop terminates at GNU
  as 2.47's layout. Fixed-width LEB128 encoders are shared with RISC-V in
  `elf_writer_common`.

Test infrastructure
- `scripts/check_errexit_negation.py`: under `set -e` a pipeline inverted
  with `!` never fails the script, so `! grep -q FORBIDDEN out.s` asserted
  nothing. 43 such assertions were dead; each now fails loudly. Where the
  dead assertion hid a stale premise it is corrected: the baseline legs of
  check_andn_fusion/check_copy64_ymm pin `-march=x86-64` explicitly, and
  check_cfg_leaf_param_homes accepts GCC's directive-free frameless leaf.
  Registered in ci_local.sh and CI, with a self-test.
- check_no_ub_exploitation.py: `\b` no longer matches after the backslash
  of an escape sequence (`"lw a0\nsw a1"` read as `nsw`).
- check_ext_vector_layout.sh uses the pinned Clang oracle
  (lib/clang_oracle.sh) and FAILS when it is missing.
- The sqrt gates encode -fmath-errno: the instruction inline plus the
  `sqrt` call kept as the errno path (GCC 16.2's shape); -fno-math-errno
  leaves the instruction alone.
- check_phi_acyclic_order.sh pins the measured 51 instructions / 3 stack
  references; against the legacy arm only strict dominance is required.
- scripts/mca_loop_ab.py: A/B the straight-line inner loops of two
  assembly trees under llvm-mca.
- ci_local.sh: a duplicate `tls-runtime-oracle` registration is removed
  (the gate still runs once).
