Subject: [PATCH 15/38] Emitter transient evictions, sret retargeting with
 GCC's NRV side conditions, atomic16 call points, SROA writer count

- x86 emitter: a sharer of a clobbered home whose last use is the
  clobbering instruction (an in-place `addq %off, %home`) must be evicted
  for the rest of that instruction, but the sticky mark used to reach blocks
  entered from paths where the home is intact. A homed-only value then had
  no recovery ("stale home with no slot/remat/acc recovery" in zstd_fast,
  zstd_lazy, zstdmt). Such evictions are recorded and settled when emission
  leaves the point; values live out across non-contiguous edges and
  same-value class members are never settled. A new default-empty
  `begin_ir_point` codegen hook announces each point (also on MachInst
  replay). 4 unit tests.
- sret_copy_elision, Phase 1 (return-slot retargeting) carries GCC's
  `dest_safe_for_nrv_p` side conditions: exact-size allocas; destination
  alignment >= source; no mention of the destination between the call and
  the copy; no escape that can execute before the call (`d = f(&d)` and
  `p = &d; d = f()` are declined); no returns-twice call. Rule 1 also
  requires `%D`'s alloca to dominate the call, the use-before-def found by
  #931's verifier sweep (the inliner materialises a callee-local
  destination AFTER the sret call in the same block): by index in the same
  block, by the dominator tree otherwise, reusing the CFG that Rules 4/5
  build. #931's separate `alloca_def` map, dominator pass and accepted-window
  list are subsumed; Rule 4 already declines a second call retargeted at
  `%D` inside the window. #931's unit tests run against this version, plus
  two cross-block ones: an entry-block destination is retargeted from its
  successor, and one on a diamond arm is not. Deleting the rule fails both
  decline tests; swapping the dominator query fails the retarget test. Phase 2 forwards
  a whole-object copy into the loads it owns, with block-local attribution.
- aggregate_sroa: every copy into the root counts as a writer at any
  offset. `d = s; d.in = n; return d.in.x` used to forward the read to
  `s.in.x`, past the member copy. A test and a positive control.
- liveness (miscompile fix): 16-byte atomics are call points of every kind
  (libatomic, or a stack-staging `lock cmpxchg16b`); narrower atomics stay
  inline. Before, -O2 homed values in %r10/%r11 across
  `call __atomic_*_16@PLT` and the peephole deleted their definitions
  (wrong results at -O2 only). check_atomic16_runtime.sh checks values live
  across each kind against GCC 16.2's output; a liveness unit test pins the
  kinds.
- narrow_copy_fold never renames %rbp into a memory operand (it is the
  frame-pointer evidence the GPR proof keys on); dead_writes keeps an LEA
  whose register is read only inside an address.
