From 7e08cb064149f7f60aaa41e7d35feae17f02fd43 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-agent <agent@arena.invalid>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 20:30:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 16/38] aggregate_sroa: resolve runtime-offset pointers to
 their object

The derivation map recorded only constant-offset GEPs, so `&s.v[k]`
resolved to itself, an apparently unrelated object, and every identity
question Transform 1 (load forwarding through a single-copy alloca) asks got
the wrong answer. All four shapes were miscompiles:

  src_store  memcpy d <- s; s.v[k] = X; load d.v[i]
             the store did not dirty the source; the load read X.
  dst_store  memcpy d <- s; d.v[k] = X; load d.v[i]
             d did not look stored-to; the load was forwarded to s.
  dst_read   memcpy d <- s; load d.v[k]; load d.v[0]
             the runtime read was not counted, every counted read was
             forwarded, and the "dead" copy was deleted under it.
  escape     memcpy d <- s; clobber(&s.v[k]); load d.v[i]
             s did not escape through the call.

Runtime-offset GEPs are now recorded with offset `None`. `resolve` returns
`(root, None)` for any chain with a runtime step: identity questions
(escape, writes reaching the source, reads of the object) see `root`, while
forwarding and splitting refuse a pointer that names no field. Every load of
the object counts toward `forwarded == loads`, whatever its offset.
`split.rs` refuses objects reached through a runtime offset.

#931's `byval.rs` consumes the same contract: a use of D through a `None`
offset declines the candidate ("variable-offset GEP", the verdict the GEP
itself already produces; blocks are visited in layout order, so the use can
be seen first), and the copy's own operands must resolve to offset
`Some(0)`.

Regression tests (outputs from GCC 16.2):
- agg_sroa_runtime_index_identity.c: the four shapes above.
- agg_copy_fwd_source_store_after.c: a plain store into a forwarded copy's
  source after the copy (same-block, cross-block, loop-carried), which #890's
  phase 2 re-rooted past.
- sret_slot_aliases_argument.c: `x = make(&x)` must not be retargeted (the
  callee may write its return slot before re-reading `*p`; GCC's
  `dest_safe_for_nrv_p`).
