From 74be571862e5164bc6bba2d16537ab3ae188e751 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-agent <agent@arena.invalid>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 01:49:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 27/38] x86 emitter: index the def matcher per function; no TLS
 walk without TLS

get_defining_instruction scanned the whole function per question: the
matcher must, because a second definition voids the first (the AMBIGUITY
LAW). Its consumers ask per access. The TLS direct-access resolver asks for
every load and store; remat and the call-argument chains ask per use. That
made emission quadratic in the function size. On zstd's huf_compress.c,
whose FORCE_INLINE bodies now inline into 2,000-instruction functions,
tls_symbol_access made 19,254 such scans (0.96G Ir) in a TU with no
thread-local variable at all.

* tls_symbol_access returns None at once when the TU has no TLS symbol. The
  walk can only answer at a GlobalAddr naming one, so this is exact.
* The matcher's answers are indexed per function. The index is built on the
  first question of a function, so functions that never ask pay nothing, and
  reset in the prologue next to current_func. It maps each value to the
  position of its unique definition, or to "defined more than once". The
  scan and the index share one classification, matched_definition, so they
  cannot drift. The index remembers the function it was built for; a
  current_func re-pointed without the reset falls back to the scan rather
  than answering for another function.

Test: on both matcher fixtures (every def arm plus a Store; the
multiply-defined loop-carried copy), the index returns the same instruction,
by identity, or the same refusal as the scan, for every value id.
