From 91de2ef71e512a6e27a0b4430579a593b81db1ae Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-agent <agent@arena.invalid>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 08:21:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 28/38] x86 peephole: stop re-solving whole functions per
 candidate

Inlining FORCE_INLINE bodies produces 2,000-line functions (zstd
huf_compress.c), and several peephole passes did O(function) work per
candidate or per fold. None of these changes alters a decision: each
replacement answers exactly what the old computation answered. Asm is
byte-identical against the previous build over the 1,044 regression-corpus
files that compile and all 40 zstd library sources at -O2.

* fuse_load_lea_into_base built a fresh FileLiveness per candidate and
  left a dead refresh_at behind. It now builds one per call and keeps it
  exact with refresh_span(i, k) after each fold. Only i, j and k change,
  and the lines between them are nops.
* coalesce_copy_into_rmw re-solved the whole file to test a rewrite. It
  now clones the solution and splices the edited span (refresh_span),
  which falls back to a one-function re-solve, never the whole file.
  FileLiveness derives Clone for this.
* LazyDeadness gains invalidate_span(lo, hi): an exact, eager splice
  at the edit, instead of a deferred whole-function refresh_at. The
  splice reads the pre-edit solution outside the span, so it cannot be
  batched. A function already queued by invalidate() is left to that full
  re-solve. fold_lea_into_memory_op (2.84 G Ir of refreshes) reports its
  i..=j / i..=k edits this way; the other callers keep invalidate().
  Its function lookup uses the solution's recorded bounds (O(1)) before
  the text scan; both name the same line.
* eliminate_move_relays re-solved the function per fold (refresh_at(j))
  although a fold changes only i and j > i: it now uses refresh_span(i, j).
* RbpRoles memoises rbp_is_gpr_in_function per function for the
  producer-retarget and move-relay passes. The verdict reads only lines
  inside its function, and every fold reports itself through edited(),
  which drops exactly that function's entry. A hit is therefore the
  predicate on the current text. The range comes from FileLiveness (O(1)),
  falling back to function_range.
* rbp_is_gpr_in_function skips lines that cannot trigger any rule using
  one substring search. Every rule needs a `bp` spelling, a leave/enter
  mnemonic or a numeric .cfi_def_cfa_register; a line with none of these
  costs one search instead of fifteen.
* family_private_in (the whole-function uniqueness proof) first walks
  outward from the candidate on the cached reg_refs bitmask alone. The
  proof is a conjunction over lines, so any non-owned mention decides
  false whatever the visiting order. The dead-write passes ask it for
  families the dataflow already calls live (62k queries), where such a
  mention is nearly always close by. The full text scan is unchanged
  and runs only when no such mention exists.

Callgrind, zstd huf_compress.c -O2 (whole compile): 33.72 G -> 23.64 G Ir
before the family_private change; final figures in the series notes.

Tests (relay_and_lea::rbp_role_and_span_tests):
* rbp_verdict_sees_every_frame_pointer_rule: one fixture per rule,
  including the bp-free spellings the prefilter must pass;
* rbp_roles_memo_forgets_only_the_edited_function;
* lazy_deadness_span_refresh_equals_a_fresh_analysis: every
  (line, family) answer equals a fresh analysis, both direct and when
  the function is already queued.
