From 416236e31f53a899ccf5e7e3161a7abdfdb7264d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-agent <agent@arena.invalid>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 09:18:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 30/38] peephole: build lea_narrow's liveness on demand and
 splice-refresh it

`narrow_lea_truncations` (e7156981b) solved whole-file `FileLiveness` on
entry, and it runs from four call sites, three inside fixpoint rounds.  Most
rounds reach no candidate that needs Rule 5 (same-register narrowing never
asks), so the solve was usually thrown away.  The solution is now built at the
first Rule-5 query; one built later from the current text is exactly what the
eager build reached through its per-fold refreshes, because each refresh
re-solved the edited function.

After a fold only line `i` (rewritten) and line `j` (deleted) changed and the
window lines between them are untouched, which is `refresh_span`'s contract,
so the fold re-splices that span instead of re-solving the function
(`CCC_VERIFY_LIVENESS_SPAN=1` checks the splice against a full solve).

Evidence: huf_compress.c -O2 under Callgrind, 19.210 G -> 19.159 G Ir
(peephole 8.924 G -> 8.873 G; FileLiveness::analyse_function -27.7 M),
object files byte-identical; corpus -O2 asm identical in every file this
change can reach (the only 4 differences in that sweep belong to the
module-symbol-facts commit).
