Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

8584329d8 introduced `ModuleSymbolFacts` as "one shared answer to 'may these
two objects be distinct?'", but the answer was still hand-rolled three times
with two different strictness levels (`vectorize`: one private definition;
`loop_idiom` and `aggregate_pair_roundtrip`: both private), and the witness
it used ("internal linkage") is stronger than the soundness argument needs.
That stopped two *public* arrays defined in the same executable from being
told apart:

    float a[N], b[N];
    void f(void) { for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) a[i] += b[i]; }

8584329d8 made `f` carry a runtime overlap check, a full scalar fallback loop
and a dead epilogue; before it, and with GCC 16.2 -O2 (whose very-cheap cost
model never versions for aliasing, so it vectorizes only on proof), `f` is the
plain 4-wide loop.

The witness is now GCC's `symtab_node::equal_address_to` rule without its
alias-oracle shortcut (which assumes *any* two variables differ): a name whose
strong (not weak, not common) definition is in this unit, binds locally
(`common::tls::binds_locally`, so the TLS model and this proof cannot drift)
and is not redirected by an alias or asm label here.  Nothing else can reach
that storage: GNU C requires an alias to be defined in its target's unit, so
every alias of it is in `aliased_names`, and local binding excludes
interposition.  Declarations never witness (another unit may alias them; the
cross-TU `loop_idiom_extern_alias` regression still runs), -fPIC public data
does not (interposable), protected data in shared-library code does not (copy
relocation, GCC's `extern_protected_data` on x86), and opaque top-level asm
still clears everything.  Redeclarations are merged per name as
`tls::model_table` merges them, which also closes a hole in the old filter: a
weak/common mark on a *separate record* of the name did not remove it.

`ModuleSymbolFacts::globals_distinct(a, b)` (a != b, neither redirected, one
witness) is the single predicate all three consumers call; `loop_idiom`
threads the facts object instead of two raw sets.

Evidence (s108a = rebased series, s108b = this):
* corpus -O2 asm: 1044 identical, 4 changed, 12 fail in both (as before).
  The 4 are exactly public-global copies/maps: loop_idiom_copy_basic
  (0 -> 6 memcpy, 607 -> 429 lines), loop_memset_norewrite (its `copyloop`,
  documented as "loop_idiom's memcpy job"), loop_idiom_copy_norewrite (the
  inlined `copy_p2p(G2, G1)`), vecreg_map_liveout (overlap check gone).
  All four match GCC 16.2 output at runtime.
* check_loop_idiom.sh: 7 new decisions (public/exec memcpy; public/-fPIC no
  memcpy + overlap-safe scalar copy kept; hidden/-fPIC memcpy; declaration vs
  definition memcpy; two declarations no memcpy + scalar kept).  s108a fails
  exactly the 3 positive ones; the negative ones hold on both.
* unit tests: witness classification (exec vs shlib, hidden declaration,
  weak/common redeclaration records, merged visibility, protected data,
  top-level asm) and `globals_distinct`'s truth table; vectorize gains the
  one-witness case.
